  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Transition Zone",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Transition Zone\"**\n\nThe screen is a triathlon transition area: blue-tinted asphalt, one thermoplastic yellow line, the agent's gear on one side and the athlete's watch and calendar on the other. Everything is read from distance, so signage is huge, condensed and capitalised, and body copy is set for legibility first. The ground is dark and grainy; objects on it (number tags, the yellow card, chat bubbles) are flat painted or printed material, lifted only by soft ambient shadow.\n\nColour is never decoration. Each hue is a material with one job: paint for rules and limits, cobalt for the agent, red for penalties and misses, Tyvek for sessions and data. If an element carries no role or state, it stays asphalt and grey.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Full-bleed grainy asphalt ground; three columns (in, line, out) with the line as a fixed-width gutter.",
      "Big Shoulders signage caps for every heading, label and number; Atkinson Hyperlegible Next for prose; JetBrains Mono only for tool output.",
      "Tyvek tags with dark numerals as the unit of data.",
      "Dashed paint-white dividers, like marked bays.",
      "Motion is literal: tags cross the line, the card snaps onto it."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Role-Only Rule",
        "body": "Colour encodes a role or a state. Yellow is a rule or limit, cobalt is the agent, red is a penalty or miss, Tyvek is a session or data point. Anything else is asphalt and grey.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Agent Colour Rule",
        "body": "Cobalt appears only where the agent speaks or acts. Never use it for links, buttons or decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Signage Rule",
        "body": "Every heading, label, number and state is Big Shoulders in caps; every sentence is Atkinson in sentence case. Numbers use tabular figures.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Machine Voice Rule",
        "body": "Mono is reserved for literal tool output and config keys. Never set prose or UI labels in mono.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Objects-Only Shadow Rule",
        "body": "Shadows belong to things that physically lie on the asphalt (tags, the card, the watch). Panels, rows and chips are flat tone steps.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do give every coloured element a role: paint for rules, cobalt for the agent, red for misses, Tyvek for sessions and data.",
      "Do set headings, labels and numbers in Big Shoulders caps with tabular figures, and prose in Atkinson Hyperlegible Next.",
      "Do highlight exactly one span per tool output with the yellow underpaint.",
      "Do honour reduced motion: transitions and animations collapse to instant.",
      "Do keep focus visible with a 3px paint outline at 3px offset."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use cobalt for anything that is not the agent.",
      "Don't use yellow as decoration; it always means a rule, a limit, or the accepted path.",
      "Don't set prose or labels in mono, or headings in the body face.",
      "Don't add shadows to flat panels, rows or chips, and never use hard offset shadows."
    ]
  }
}